Withings' changes to the nrfx 2.1.0 SAADC driver, recovered from appl v3411.

The base is nrfx 2.1.0 (2.2.0's nrfx_saadc.c differs only in the sign of one
NRFX_ASSERT in nrfx_saadc_limits_set, a body the image does not carry, so the
two are indistinguishable here). It is not the nrfx the rest of the build
uses: nRF5 SDK 17.1.0 ships nrfx 1.9.0, whose SAADC driver is the old
channel_init/buffer_convert API, and matching that API's bodies is what made
the driver look unrecoverable. The timer-driven mode with SAMPLERATE and
OVERSAMPLE and the two-buffer EasyDMA handover the sim work found are stock
nrfx 2.x advanced mode, not a Withings addition.

The already-configured test shifts an unsigned value. channels_configured is a
uint8_t, so `>> ch` promotes it to int and GCC emits `asr`; the image has `lsr`
at 0x7dd60. The two are the same arithmetic on a value that cannot be negative,
so nothing but the cast decides which instruction comes out, and with it one
more byte of nrfx_saadc_channels_config agrees.

Build, measured the same way: GCC 13.2.Rel1 (the toolchain that also builds
the image's newlib 4.3.0.20230120) at -Os, without -fno-builtin, and
abi/config-relink/nrfx_glue.h for the two glue macros. Under that
configuration thirteen of the driver's fourteen bodies reproduce the image
byte for byte once the relocated fields are masked (abi/body_check.py); the
one that does not is discussed at the end.

Hunk 1, nrfx_saadc.h, the channel descriptor gains an idle input pair.
  nrfx_saadc_channel_t grows from 10 bytes to 12: two nrf_saadc_input_t
  fields before pin_p/pin_n, so channel_index moves from offset 9 to 11.
  The image's stride is 12 (`movs r2,#0xc; mla r1,r2,r6,r7` in
  nrfx_saadc_channels_config) and it reads the index at +0xb, the idle pair
  at +7/+8 and the active pair at +9/+10.

Hunk 2, saadc_generic_mode_set, the activation test is a right shift.
  `(ch_to_activate_mask >> ch_pos) & 1` instead of
  `ch_to_activate_mask & (1 << ch_pos)`: the image computes `lsr.w r3,r0,r2;
  ands r3,r3,#1` where the release computes `lsl.w r3,r6,r2; ands r3,r3,r0`.
  No behaviour changes; with this hunk the body is byte-identical.

Hunk 3, nrfx_saadc_channels_config, configure parks the channel and
activation connects it.
  The release records pin_p/pin_n in the control block and leaves the
  hardware PSELP/PSELN alone until saadc_generic_mode_set activates the
  channel. The image programs PSELN then PSELP at configure time from the
  new idle pair, and records the active pair in the control block.
  What the caller does with it (0x7e2e4, the ECG/battery channel table)
  is the point: it passes the idle pair as DISABLED/DISABLED and the real
  input in pin_p, so the analog input is disconnected from the moment the
  channel is configured until the moment it is activated.
  The already-configured test is the same right shift as hunk 2, and the
  index is lifted into a local, which the image's register allocation shows.
  NRFX_ASSERT(pin_p) stays. This hunk once wrapped the three control-block
  stores in `if (p_channels[i].pin_p)` instead, on the reading that a channel
  with no active pin should be parked and left unmarked; the bytes refuse it.
  The image branches the other way at 0x7de04 -- `cmp.w lr, #0; bne` into the
  stores -- so pin_p equal to zero falls into the assert's `udf #0` at 0x7dd20,
  and it is the assert arm, not a loop continue, because the block before it
  writes channels_configured back the way both return paths do. With the assert
  restored the body's 292 bytes agree with the image; with the `if` 73 of 280
  differed, the pointer increment among them (the image hoists `adds r3, #0xc`
  above the PSELP store and reads the rest of the record at negative offsets,
  which is what the unconditional stores let it do).

Hunk 4, nrfx_saadc_mode_trigger, the blocking simple mode stops before it
disables.
  After clearing END the image triggers TASKS_STOP and spins on
  EVENTS_STOPPED before writing ENABLE = 0; the release disables directly.
  This is the path the battery read takes.

The one body that does not reproduce is saadc_event_limits_handle, 57 of 92
bytes, and it is not a semantic difference: the same instructions in a
different order. The image clears the bit first (`lsl.w r3,r6,r2; bic.w
r4,r4,r3`) and only then computes the event offset (`lsl.w r3,r2,#3`); ours
computes `lsls r0,r2,#3` before the compare and does the bic after the `ite`,
and the register assignment follows.

The pass that does it is code hoisting. nrf_saadc_limit_event_get returns
LIMITL or LIMITH, both `base + channel * 8`, so GCC lifts the multiply to the
block that dominates the two arms, which is above the bit clear. With
-fno-code-hoisting the 92 bytes agree exactly. That is not the image's build:
the same flag breaks nrfx_saadc_mode_trigger in this very file (291 of 336)
and prvAddNewTaskToReadyList in the kernel (10 of 256), both of which
reproduce with hoisting on, so neither a build flag nor a `#pragma GCC
optimize` over the file can be the explanation -- only a per-function
`__attribute__((optimize("no-code-hoisting")))`, which reproduces it and which
nobody writes.

No source edit reproduces it either. Measured against the body: the statement
order of the bit clear and the event get (either way round, and with the clear
moved to the bottom of the loop), the clear written as `^=`, as `~(1U << ch)`
and through a separate bit local, `channel` as uint32_t and as volatile, a
volatile local for limits_activated, `__builtin_expect` on the event test both
ways, hot, cold, optimize O1/O2/O3/Os and noinline on the function, the loop as
a do/while, an NRFX_ASSERT or a memory barrier between the clear and the get,
and the event offset written as one expression in the HAL or open-coded in the
driver. Every one of them leaves the same 57 bytes or makes the body worse.
The order is the compiler's, and the rest of the file pins the compiler.

The -f flags previously tried and rejected against the full body set:
-fno-if-conversion, -fno-if-conversion2, -fno-schedule-insns2,
-fira-algorithm=priority, -fira-region, -fsched-pressure, -fno-caller-saves,
-fno-gcse, -funroll-loops, -fno-tree-ter, -O1/-O2/-O3, and GCC 12.3, 13.3
and 14.2.

